==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|corium|lang=la||leather}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|coria}}

# {{context|anatomy|lang=en}} The inner layer of [[skin]], the [[dermis]].
# {{context|anatomy|lang=en}} The deep layer of [[mucous membrane]]s beneath the [[epithelium]].
# {{context|historical|lang=en}} [[armour|Armour]] made of [[leather]], particularly that used by the [[Roman]]s.
#: {{rfquotek|Fosbroke}}

===Etymology 2===
{{suffix|core|ium|lang=en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{context|nuclear physics|lang=en}} The [[lavalike]] material produced in a nuclear [[meltdown]].
#* {{cite-book|year=1991|title=Numerical modelling of basic heat transfer phenomena in nuclear systems|author=Franklin Chung and L.E. Hochreiter|page=32|passage=Previous studies of the thermal behavior of '''corium''' in a degraded nuclear reactor have focussed primarily on the process of heat transfer within the '''corium'''.}}
#* '''2009''', Wei Wei and Xin-rong Cao, "The Simulation of '''Corium''' Dispersion in Direct Containment Heating Accidents", ''Zero Carbon Energy Kyoto 2009''.
#* '''2011''', C. Journeau and M. Ficsher, ''Nuclear Safety in Light Water Reactors: Severe Accident Phenomenology'', page 569:
#*: As a result, dedicated core catchers have been designed that can gather the '''corium''' and cool it safely.

